.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md-8-## Key takeaways
.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md-9-
.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md-10-- The pinned SwiftNIO SSH baseline is tag `0.14.1`, commit
.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md-11-  `31cdc3c3391a10460dedf1170530cf651d2ca496`. Its exact resolved graph builds in
.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md:12:  release mode on macOS. It fixes the critical NIOSSH ECDSA advisory and resolves
.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md-13-  to patched Swift Crypto and SwiftNIO versions.
.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md-14-- At that source, every newly opened child channel receives a hard-coded
.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md:15:  `1 << 24` byte (16 MiB) target receive window. There is no public setter or
.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md:16:  observation hook. The client-initiated `_rekey()` method is also `internal`.
.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md-17-  Both require source-fork work and validation; neither gate may be waived.
.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md-18-- The most recent libssh2 release, `1.11.1`, is affected by six advisories fixed
.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md-19-  after that release. The auditable candidate baseline is therefore the exact,
.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md-20-  untagged upstream commit
.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md-21-  `a34302491c164d53c900fec9b3cbb050ecebe719` (`1.11.2_DEV`) with all six fixes,
.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md-22-  paired with OpenSSL `3.5.7`. This removes known release vulnerabilities but
.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md-23-  creates an explicit release-hygiene and supply-chain risk.
.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md-24-- libssh2 publicly exposes initial per-channel windows, remaining-window
.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md-25-  observation, and manual window adjustment. Its direct-tcpip convenience API
.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md-26-  still hardcodes the 2 MiB default, so a custom-window adapter must use the
.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md-27-  generic channel-open API and reproduce the RFC 4254 payload.
.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md-28-- libssh2 handles server-initiated KEX internally but exposes no public API for
.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md:29:  client-initiated rekey and no local byte/time threshold. Consequently both
.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md:30:  candidates fail the public client-rekey source gate before adaptation and both
.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md-31-  require the same sustained-transfer experiment.
.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md-32-- Neither source inspection nor upstream claims establish 500–1,000 channel
.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md-33-  headroom, extension-safe Apple packaging, bounded physical memory, or robust
.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md-34-  cancellation. These remain named, symmetric experiments. This audit does not
.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md-35-  select an engine.
.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md-36-
